================================================================================
`elephant-split 2 execute` — the cheap discriminator.  🔴 MY PREDICTION WAS WRONG.
================================================================================
  leaf instructions   2,114,787,163      (was 2,395,132,641 on the 2.0.0 stack)
  executor wall             15.71 s
  saving                   −11.70%

PREDICTED BEFORE THE RUN: −14.5%, band 13–15%.  MEASURED −11.70%, outside the band.

What I did wrong, precisely.  I had refitted the monolithic elephant law and then
*derived* the leaf from it instead of measuring it — while a 15-second `execute` was
available the whole time.  The derivation assumed the leaf-to-marginal gap is a constant
across the release:
    leaf_old − marginal_old = 2,395,132,641 − 2.4627e9 = −67,567,359
    ⇒ leaf_new = 2.1148e9 − 67,567,359 = 2,047,232,641   (−14.5%)
The other natural hypothesis, a constant *ratio* (0.97255 × 2.1148e9 = 2,056,750,940),
predicts −14.1%.  BOTH are wrong and both err the same way.  I stated only one of them
and gave it a two-point band, which made a guess look like a bracket.

This is "refit, never divide" one level further in than I applied it: I refitted the law
and then scaled something else by it.  A quantity is only measured where it is measured.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, and it is a sharper fact than the prediction was:
  leaf_new / marginal_new = 0.999994 — they coincide to 6 parts per million.
  leaf_old / marginal_old = 0.97255.
The two are different ELFs (`program-leaf-elephant` vs `program-elephant`) and the
marginal term is fitted from the monolithic one at n=1,2,4, so this is not an artefact of
shared arithmetic.  Restated as the difference: the monolithic per-response path shed
347.9M instructions (−14.13%) and the leaf shed 280.3M (−11.70%); the 67.6M between them
is exactly the old gap.  So ~67.6M instructions per response left the monolithic
program's per-response path without leaving the leaf's.

🔎 OPEN, NOT CHASED.  That is a specific, findable 3%-of-a-leaf difference and it is out
of scope for bringing the stack up to date.  Recorded with its number so it can be picked
up deliberately rather than rediscovered.

⚠️ Note against the time figures: the monolithic marginal fell 14.13% in INSTRUCTIONS
while `lift 219` fell 3.58% in SECONDS.  Those are the hardened-child and elephant
programs respectively, so they are not the same quantity and must not be compared — but
it is a standing reminder that an instruction count is not a proving time, because a
release can change the instruction MIX (precompile calls vs ALU) as well as the count.

================================================================================
CONTROL: does proving TIME track instruction COUNT across this release?  NO.
================================================================================
Recovered from `git show HEAD:host/src/bin/elephant_probe.rs` — the two embedded
constants deleted earlier today, which are the only surviving record of the 2.0.0-era
hardened-child cost:
    HASH_FULL_INSTRUCTIONS = 4.775013465e9      HASH_FULL_SECONDS = 10,296.11

Against today's measured pair (child law refitted on this run, 3.0274e7 + 1.9889e7·n
⇒ 4.3861e9 at 219; `lift 219` proving 9,927.49 s):

    instructions at 219   4.775013465e9 → 4.3861e9      −8.14%
    proving seconds          10,296.11  → 9,927.49      −3.58%
    effective rate          463,769/s   → 441,791/s     −4.74%  ← got WORSE

So the release removed 8.1% of the guest's instructions and recovered only 3.6% of the
wall clock, because it also changed the instruction MIX.  **An instruction saving must
not be quoted as a time saving on this stack** — it is measurably less than half of one.

⚠️ One input is not independently verifiable.  HASH_FULL_SECONDS is confirmed against
fixtures/lift-219-gpusafe.summary.log:18; HASH_FULL_INSTRUCTIONS is an embedded constant
whose ELF no longer exists, so it cannot be re-derived.  Two things support it: it is
co-provenanced with the seconds figure in the same commit, and it is internally
consistent with a law — implied old fixed 31.47e6 / marginal 21.66e6 against today's
30.27e6 / 19.89e6, both moving by small sane amounts (−3.8%, −8.2%).  Supporting, not
proof.  The −8.14% carries that one caveat; the −3.58% does not.

🪤 Do not conflate the two marginals: the hardened child's fell 8.18%, the elephant's
14.13%.  They are different statements and neither predicts the other.

================================================================================
PREDICTIONS for `elephant-split 2 both`, written BEFORE the run.  ~6.5 h expected.
================================================================================
Baseline (2.0.0 stack): leaf 4,207.74 s / 73.4 GB; root over 2 leaves 15,589.86 s /
74.4 GB; two leaves plus a root = 24,005 s = 6.67 h.

LEAF, point estimate 3,890 s.  Chain: measured −11.70% in instructions (above), times
the +4.74% rate degradation just measured on the child.  🚨 That second factor is
borrowed from a DIFFERENT STATEMENT, which is the exact move this project has a standing
rule against.  It is used because it is the only measurement of the release's effect on
time that exists, and it is declared rather than hidden.
  Band: 3,700 s (instructions alone, no degradation) to 4,050 s (degradation twice the
  child's).  Anything outside that band is a finding, not noise.

ROOT, point estimate 15,300 s, band 15,100–15,600 s, i.e. essentially unchanged.
Reasoning: the root is ~79% the one-off secp256k1 fingerprint traversal, and that
traversal is the elephant law's FIXED term, which moved 5.1004e9 → 5.0986e9 = −0.035%.
A root that moves materially would mean the fixed term does not describe it.

TOTAL, point estimate 23,100 s ≈ 6.4 h.  Peak RSS predicted unchanged at ~74 GB: nothing
in this release changes how much proof data crosses the guest (frame size is measured
identical at 3.451 MB/response, and 0.64 GB at 219).

Also predicted: the nine-case gate green with the control accepting, and the root journal
through the relying-party gate.
